    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Company and the Nawab each had aims: the Company wanted recognition of its privileges and reparation for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, and Siraj-ud-Daulah wanted to stop the unauthorised fortifications and the loss of his revenue.</li><li>The Company's side won with a small force because of a conspiracy: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>, Rai Durlabh and Yar Lutuf Khan brought their troops to the field but made no move, and the Nawab fled.</li><li>To deal with the merchant Omichund, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Clive</a> had two treaties drawn up, a real one on white paper and a false one on red, and Admiral Watson's signature on the false one was counterfeited.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Clive</a>'s two treaties, a real one on white paper and a false one on red to deceive Omichund, were signed by the Company's committee, but Admiral Watson signed only the real one, and his signature on the other had to be counterfeited.</p></aside>
    </section>

