    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">April 1756</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan">Alivardi Khan</a> dies and is succeeded by his grandson Siraj-ud-Daulah, aged twenty-three</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1756</span><span class="rf-text">Siraj-ud-Daulah takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, and many of the prisoners die in the Black Hole</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1757</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Clive</a> and Admiral Watson recapture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> on 2 January</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1757</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Clive</a> takes the French town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandannagar">Chandannagar</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1757</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> signs the treaties with the Company on 4 June</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1757</span><span class="rf-text">The battle is fought on 23 June; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> is saluted as Nawab the next day</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">July 1757</span><span class="rf-text">Siraj-ud-Daulah is betrayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmahal">Rajmahal</a> on 2 July and murdered that night by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>'s son Miran's order</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1759</span><span class="rf-text">The Company defeats a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> force at Chinsura and replaces <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-plassey" data-v="814f4bdae2" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies take their ground</b> The Nawab's army formed an arc with the French guns at the larger tank in front. Clive lined up the Europeans in the centre with a sepoy division on each side, and posted four guns at the kilns to answer the French.</li><li><b>The guns open and Clive withdraws to the grove</b> The French guns fired the first shot and the rest of the Nawab's artillery followed. The British lost 10 Europeans and 20 sepoys by 8:30, and Clive left the advanced guns at the kilns and drew the army back into the grove.</li><li><b>Rain, and Mir Madan Khan's charge is driven back</b> A rainstorm drenched the Nawab's gunpowder, while the British covered theirs. Mir Madan Khan led his cavalry in a charge, but British grapeshot mortally wounded him and drove his men back.</li><li><b>The Nawab's army turns back to its entrenchments</b> The Nawab's army stopped its cannonade and began turning back north to its entrenchments. This left St. Frais and his artillery without support.</li><li><b>Kilpatrick advances and the British take the larger tank</b> Major Kilpatrick advanced on his own with two companies and two field pieces towards the French position. Clive brought up the rest of the army behind him, and the French artillery retreated to the redoubt.</li><li><b>Mir Jafar's division turns towards the grove and is halted</b> The Nawab's left arm, which had lingered behind the rest, turned and marched towards the grove. Clive took it for a threat to his baggage and sent three platoons and a field piece, whose fire halted it.</li><li><b>The British take the hill and the redoubt</b> Clive ordered a three-pronged attack on the redoubt and the hill east of it. Major Coote's grenadiers took the hill at 16:30, the redoubt fell after St. Frais retreated, and by 17:00 the British held the entrenchment and the camp.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan.jpg" alt="Alivardi Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan">Alivardi Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darbar_of_Alivardi_Khan_at_Murshidabad%27s_Court_MET_DP-19635-001_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-subah">Bengal Subah</a> had been the largest subdivision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and later an independent state under the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. The Company held its stations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, and followed a policy of allying with princes and Nawabs in return for concessions. The French also had stations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandannagar">Chandannagar</a> in Bengal, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnatic-wars">Carnatic Wars</a> brought British and French into proxy wars among local rulers.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan">Alivardi Khan</a> had allowed the Europeans to strengthen their fortifications during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> invasions, but also took large sums from them. The Company complained that it could not enjoy fully the farman of 1717, and its passes for native traders and duties on goods hurt the Nawab's revenue. Alivardi died in April 1756 and his twenty-three-year-old grandson Siraj-ud-Daulah, said to have had a ferocious temper, was suspicious of the Europeans' profits and ordered them to stop fortifying. When the British refused, he took Cossimbazar and then Calcutta, which fell on 20 June 1756, its garrison of about 500 facing a force of nearly 50,000 infantry and cavalry.</p><p>Prisoners were put into the dungeon of Fort William, the Black Hole, which held 146 and where only 23 walked out the next day. It appears that the Nawab did not know of these conditions. The Company's council at Madras sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Clive</a> and Admiral Watson to re-establish the settlements, obtain recognition of its privileges and reparation for its losses. They retook Calcutta on 2 January 1757.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Plassey&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Carnatic Wars, Bengal campaign, Conspiracy:</p><ul><li>S.C. Hill (ed.), Bengal in 1756–1757 (John Murray, 1905)</li><li>Peter Harrington, Plassey 1757, Clive of India's Finest Hour; Osprey Campaign Series #36 (Osprey Publishing, 1994)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mir-jafar.jpg" alt="Mir Jafar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mir_Jafar_(left)_and_Mir_Miran_(right).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Two accounts of the Nawab's strength are given. One puts his army at about 50,000, including defectors, with 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and 10 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, against 3,000 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Clive</a>. The other counts 30,000 infantry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a>, swords, pikes and rockets and 20,000 cavalry, among 300 pieces of artillery, with about 50 French artillerymen under de St. Frais, and puts the Nawab's army in an arc facing the grove, 45,000 of them apart from the 5,000 cavalry and 7,000 infantry behind the French guns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> commanded the left, closest to the British, Yar Lutuf Khan the centre and Rai Durlabh the right.</p><p>Clive's army, after joining Major Kilpatrick, had 613 Europeans, 171 artillerymen with eight field pieces and two howitzers, 91 topasses, 2,100 sepoys and 150 sailors. In the battle account it is 750 European infantry, 100 topasses, 2,100 sepoys, 100 artillerymen and 50 sailors. Its camp was the Laksha Bagh, a mango grove 800 yards long, about a mile from the Nawab's entrenchments.</p><p>Both sides wanted something other than a plain contest. Siraj-ud-Daulah, threatened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, could not send his entire force. The Company turned to a conspiracy with the Nawab's army chief Mir Jafar, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagat-seth">Jagat Seths</a>, Yar Lutuf Khan, Omichund and Rai Durlabh, who, would bring their troops but not fight.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-clive.jpg" alt="Robert Clive" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Clive,_1st_Baron_Clive_by_Nathaniel_Dance,_(later_Sir_Nathaniel_Dance-Holland,_Bt).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nathaniel Dance, (later Sir Nathaniel Dance-Holland, Bt) (died 1811), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 23 June the Nawab's army advanced from its camp towards the grove at daybreak. At 8:00 the French artillery at the larger tank fired the first shot, and the Nawab's artillery fired heavily. By 8:30 the British had lost 10 Europeans and 20 sepoys, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Clive</a> pulled back into the shelter of the grove. After three hours nothing had changed, and the British staff decided to hold until night and attack at midnight. A heavy rainstorm then drenched the Nawab's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, which was not covered, while the British protected theirs with tarpaulins.</p><p>Believing the British guns were useless, Mir Madan Khan led a cavalry charge and was mortally wounded by grapeshot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj</a>, who had stayed in his tent, was deeply disturbed and begged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> to defend his turban; Mir Jafar promised his service and sent word to Clive urging him on. Rai Durlabh advised Siraj to withdraw, and he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> with 2,000 horsemen.</p><p>At about 14:00 the Nawab's army turned back to its entrenchments. Major Kilpatrick advanced on the French position, and Clive, reprimanding him for acting without orders, led the army to take it at 15:00. Clive checked a division that turned out to be Mir Jafar's, then took the redoubt and the hill beyond it. The British held the entrenchment and the abandoned camp by 17:00.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Plassey&#8221; cites where it covers Order of battle, Battle, Battle begins, Death of Mir Madan Khan:</p><ul><li>Peter Harrington, Plassey 1757, Clive of India's Finest Hour; Osprey Campaign Series #36 (Osprey Publishing, 1994)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On the evening of 23 June <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Clive</a>, and on the next morning at Daudpur Clive embraced him and saluted him as Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. Mir Jafar reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> on 24 June, and Clive arrived on 29 June with 200 European soldiers and 300 sepoys, in the wake of rumours of an attempt on his life, and placed Mir Jafar on the throne.</p><p>Siraj-ud-Daulah reached Murshidabad at midnight on 23 June and escaped northwards by boat with his wife and jewels on the night of 24 June. He was discovered on 2 July at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmahal">Rajmahal</a>, betrayed to the local military governor, who was Mir Jafar's brother, and handed over to Mir Jafar's son Miran, who had him murdered that night. His remains were paraded through Murshidabad and buried at the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alivardi-khan">Alivardi Khan</a>.</p><p>The treaty gave the British all the land within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> Ditch and 600 yards beyond it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> of land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> and the sea, and required 22,000,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> (£2,750,000) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siraj-ud-daulah">Siraj</a>'s wealth proved far less than expected, so a council on 29 June decided that half would be paid at once. When it was revealed to Omichund that he would receive nothing, he is said to have gone insane. Clive reported 22 British killed and 50 wounded, and estimated that the Nawab lost 500 men.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Plassey&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Peter Harrington, Plassey 1757, Clive of India's Finest Hour; Osprey Campaign Series #36 (Osprey Publishing, 1994)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known largely from British works: Robert Orme's History of the Military Transactions (1861 edition), Malleson's Decisive Battles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> (1885), S. C. Hill's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1756-1757 (1905), the Memoirs of the Revolution in Bengal (1760) by John Campbell and William Watts, and Peter Harrington's Plassey 1757 (1994). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Clive</a>'s testimony to the House of Commons on 10 May 1773 is his own defence of the conspiracy: he called Omichund a great villain whom it was true policy and justice to deceive.</p><p>The figures differ. The Nawab's army appears as about 50,000, including defectors, with 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, and as 30,000 infantry and 20,000 cavalry with 300 pieces of artillery; Clive's force as 3,000, and in detail as 613 Europeans, 2,100 sepoys and others. The battle is said to have lasted approximately 11 hours, though the sequence given runs from 8:00 to about 17:00. The 500 lost by the Nawab is Clive's own estimate, and a name in his testimony, Duprée, may be a mistranscription of Dupleix.</p><p>The money is reported with attributions: the historian W. Dalrymple gives the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagat-seth">Jagat Seths</a>' offer to Clive and the Company of more than £4m and 110,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> a month for Company troops, and Omichund's demand is given as three million rupees. The battle is called decisive, yet the result is put down to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>'s betrayal, the Nawab's flight and the commitment of much of the Bengal army against the Afghan invasion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mir-qasim.jpg" alt="Mir Qasim" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mir_Qasim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Plassey was a step towards colonial control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. The British gained wealth and influence over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>, extracted concessions for earlier losses and trade income, and used the revenue to build military strength and push out other European powers. The French were no longer a significant force in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. The battle helped the Company take complete control of Bengal in 1773.</p><p>The settlement was unstable. By 1759 Mir Jafar, finding his position as a subordinate to the British intolerable, encouraged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> to advance against them. The Dutch sent seven large ships and 1,400 men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Clive</a> defeated them at Chinsura on 25 November 1759. The British then deposed Mir Jafar and installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a>. Clive returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, was made Baron Clive of Plassey in the Irish peerage and obtained a seat in the House of Commons.</p><p>The struggle continued in the south, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eyre-coote">Eyre Coote</a> defeated a French garrison in 1761, and the French, returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a> in 1763, never regained their former stature. The British would emerge as rulers of the subcontinent in subsequent years. A monument, the Palashi Monument, stands on the battlefield.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Plassey on 23 June 1757 a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-clive">Robert Clive</a> defeated the army of Nawab Siraj-ud-Daulah, whose commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a> and other officers had conspired with the Company and did not fight. Mir Jafar became Nawab, and Siraj-ud-Daulah was killed.</em></p>

